.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt JOBMARSHAL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jobmarshal
.Nd per-user build coordination service
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm add-wrapper
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm help
.Op Ar command
.Nm
.Cm list-wrappers
.Nm
.Cm print-env
.Op Fl -format Ns = Ns Ar shell|systemd
.Nm
.Cm remove-wrapper
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm run
.Op Fl -
.Ar COMMAND
.Op Ar ARG ...
.Nm
.Cm serve
.Op Fl j Ar N | Fl -jobs Ar N
.Nm
.Cm start
.Op Fl j Ar N | Fl -jobs Ar N
.Nm
.Cm stats
.Op Fl -zero
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm stop
.Nm
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages a private per-user service on Linux and FreeBSD.
The
.Cm help
and
.Cm version
commands print command-line information without accessing service configuration.
.Ss Managing wrappers
The preferred way to wrap commands is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jobmarshal add-wrapper cargo
jobmarshal add-wrapper cc
jobmarshal list-wrappers
eval "$(jobmarshal start)"
jobmarshal remove-wrapper cc
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm add-wrapper
installs or refreshes one alias without starting the daemon.
The real command need not exist yet.
.Cm list-wrappers
prints sorted names, one per line; an absent directory produces no output.
.Cm remove-wrapper
removes an alias.
Adding an existing name and removing an absent name succeed.
Names must begin with an ASCII letter or digit and contain only ASCII letters,
digits, underscores, periods, plus signs, or hyphens.
The names jobmarshal and jobmarshal-wrapper are reserved.
.Pp
Aliases live in
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/jobmarshal/bin ,
defaulting to
.Pa ~/.local/share/jobmarshal/bin .
The entire bin directory is managed: valid regular-file names define the wrapper
selection, and unrelated files must not be placed there.
Unexpected names, symlinks, and other file types are errors.
Application directories must be real directories owned by you without group or
other write access.
The data path cannot contain a colon, which separates PATH entries.
.Pp
A private copy of the installed wrapper lives in
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/jobmarshal/libexec/jobmarshal-wrapper .
Aliases are hardlinks to this user-owned copy, falling back to copies when
linking is unavailable.
Before launching a new daemon, jobmarshal compares the installed binary with the
private copy and refreshes stale aliases.
Starting an existing daemon skips refresh; stop and restart after an upgrade.
Adding a wrapper prepares the private copy and refreshes only that alias.
Updates use atomic replacement; required refresh failures prevent daemon startup.
Keep the installed management command in PATH so managed aliases can autostart it.
Configure-selected wrappers remain available as an alternative; see
.Xr jobmarshal-wrapper 1 .
.Ss Running commands
.Cm run
executes COMMAND through the installed wrapper and shared job budget,
autostarting the service if necessary.
An optional
.Fl -
may precede COMMAND.
Options and arguments after COMMAND belong to the child and are passed unchanged,
including non-UTF-8 arguments.
The command preserves the environment, working directory, standard streams,
exit status, and signals.
Nested invocations reuse inherited allocations without reserving another slot.
.Pp
The management executable locates
.Pa ../libexec/jobmarshal-wrapper
relative to itself.
This relative layout intentionally supports relocation after installation.
Custom bindir and libexecdir settings must preserve this relationship.
Keep the installed bindir in
.Ev PATH
so the wrapper can locate the management command when autostarting.
.Ss Starting the service
.Cm start
starts a detached daemon with its diagnostics in the private
.Pa daemon.log
file in the runtime directory.
It prints a shell-quoted export of
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
on standard output.
If the managed bin directory is absent from PATH, it also prints a shell-quoted
PATH export containing the complete value with that directory prepended.
An existing entry is left in its current position.
Repeated initialization does not accumulate entries, so shell startup files can use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
eval "$(jobmarshal start -j 8)"
.Ed
.Pp
The maximum job capacity comes from
.Fl j
or
.Fl -jobs ,
then
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_JOBS ,
then the number of available CPUs.
The value must be a positive decimal integer.
Starting an existing service succeeds when it is responsive and not draining.
An explicitly requested capacity must match; stop the service before changing it.
.Pp
.Cm serve
accepts the same capacity options but runs in the foreground, retains its
standard streams for diagnostics, and prints no environment assignments.
It rejects an existing daemon.
.Ss Session initialization
.Cm print-env
prints the runtime-directory and idempotent PATH assignments from
.Cm start
without starting a daemon, creating directories, or refreshing wrappers.
The default
.Fl -format=shell
uses shell-quoted exports suitable for:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
eval "$(jobmarshal print-env)"
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl -format=systemd
prints quoted assignments for systemd environment generators.
Values must be UTF-8 without newlines; unrepresentable output fails before
printing any assignments.
Both formats are available even when systemd integration is not installed.
.Ss Systemd user service
When installed, enable the user unit after stopping any detached daemon:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jobmarshal stop
systemctl --user enable jobmarshal.service
systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user start jobmarshal.service
.Ed
.Pp
The unit runs
.Cm serve ,
logs to the journal, and drains builds through
.Cm stop
without a stop timeout.
Manage it with systemctl --user stop or restart.
Set capacity through a service drop-in, for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[Service]
Environment=JOBMARSHAL_JOBS=8
.Ed
.Pp
The environment generator initializes future user-manager services.
The profile.d snippet initializes login shells that source that directory.
Both require an enabled unit; absent enablement links require only builtin
filesystem checks, while candidate links trigger an offline systemctl check
that respects masking.
Neither initializer starts the daemon.
Existing processes retain their environment; use
.Cm print-env
for the current shell or shells that do not source profile.d.
Configure defaults to automatic detection; --with-systemd requires integration
and --without-systemd disables installation of the unit and both initializers.
Installation does not enable the unit or modify personal shell files.
.Ss Inspecting and stopping the service
.Cm status
reports the daemon PID, maximum jobs, active and waiting build counts, and draining
state.
.Cm stop
rejects queued and new independent builds, waits for active builds to finish,
then acknowledges the request before complete teardown.
Nested wrappers may finish using their inherited allocation while draining.
Stable lock files remain in the runtime directory after shutdown.
.Ss Statistics
.Cm stats
reports the listening socket, token FIFO, daemon PID, UTC start and reset
timestamps, maximum jobs, available and unavailable tokens, active builds,
processed builds, and draining state.
Timestamps use UTC RFC3339 format.
The command does not start an absent service.
.Pp
Processed builds count admitted leases when they end, including failed commands.
Queued requests that never receive admission do not count.
.Fl -zero
resets the processed count and reset timestamp before printing the snapshot.
It preserves the start timestamp, active builds, and token state.
Builds active during reset count when they subsequently finish.
All statistics remain in memory and restart resets them.
.Pp
Token counts are observations that can change immediately after collection.
Unavailable tokens include implicit build reservations, native token holders,
and tokens lost by native clients; they do not measure processes or threads.
Job demand and wait duration are not measured.
Statistics observe the FIFO without consuming tokens or changing descriptor flags.
.Ss Runtime directory
The runtime directory is selected from
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR ,
then the XDG runtime directory with
.Pa jobmarshal
appended, then
.Pa /tmp/jobmarshal- Ns Ar uid
if no usable XDG runtime directory exists.
The final path must be absolute UTF-8 without whitespace.
It must be a real directory owned by the current user without group or other
permissions; a new directory is created with mode 0700.
The service rejects unsafe directories, lock files, and log files.
.Pp
Ordinary control operations have bounded connection and I/O deadlines.
Admission and draining wait for build completion without a fixed deadline.
Missing or refused sockets indicate an absent service; other failures are errors.
.Ss Shared job budget
The private token FIFO holds one token for every configured job slot.
Each independent wrapped build reserves one for its implicit slot; native GNU
make and Cargo clients share the remaining tokens.
Inherited allocations let nested wrappers run without reserving another slot.
Tokens lost by native clients reduce capacity until a safe stop and restart.
Surviving builds prevent replacement of a crashed daemon's pool until they exit.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
Explicit private runtime directory.
.It Ev JOBMARSHAL_JOBS
Explicit capacity used when the command line does not select one.
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
Persistent wrapper storage base; defaults to $HOME/.local/share.
.It Ev PATH
Command lookup and the input to PATH exports from start and print-env.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
XDG runtime base directory, accessed through the XDG library.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Cm add-wrapper ,
.Cm help ,
.Cm list-wrappers ,
.Cm print-env ,
.Cm remove-wrapper ,
.Cm serve ,
.Cm start ,
.Cm stats ,
.Cm stop ,
and
.Cm version
commands return 0 on success and 1 on execution failure.
.Cm status
returns 0 when the service responds and 1 when absent or on an error.
Usage errors, including a missing command, return 2.
For
.Cm run ,
a missing or unexecutable wrapper returns 1, wrapper setup failures return 125,
and executed children retain their exit status or terminating signal.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jobmarshal-wrapper 1
.Pp
